rank":143,"year":1969,"note":null,"total":999,"scaruffi":null},{"id":198433558933938,"name":"This album is the BEST in its GENRE @Frenchblues","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":514,"year":null,"note":null,"total":730,"scaruffi":null},{"id":85122334056025,"name":"Grammy Award: Album of the Year nominees","src":"musthear","grp":"musicbrainz","list_year":null,"rank":1970,"year":null,"note":null,"total":279,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"\"Abbey Road\" was the best-selling vinyl record of the 2010s, highlighting the format's unexpected resurgence. Its continued popularity decades after its original release in 1969 underscored a significant trend in music consumption during the decade. \"The Beatles' Abbey Road was the biggest vinyl seller of the 2010s.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of the 2010s Part 5: Music and Tech","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mNqqLDpUuZY"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The Beatles' *Abbey Road* album featured the John Lennon song \"Sun King,\" which George Harrison later admitted was inspired by Fleetwood Mac's 1969 number 1 UK hit instrumental \"Albatross.\" This served as a tribute from The Beatles to their contemporaries. \"Later that same year, the Beatles paid tribute on their Abbey Road album with the very Albatross-like John Lennon song Sun King.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Thinking About Tomorrow Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The EMI Studios officially changed its name to Abbey Road in 1970, after this album was released by The Beatles, capitalizing on the massive publicity. The studio's entrance and the famous crosswalk are visible on the album's front cover. \"It stayed with the name EMI Studios until 1970, when the Beatles, their most famous clients, released an album called Abbey Road.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Legendary Recording Studios","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N5kSZOl-5UY"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The song \"Here Comes the Sun\" from this album is identified as the most streamed of all Beatles songs. \"His song Here Comes the Sun from the Abbey Road album is the most streamed of all Beatles songs.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"60 Mind Blowing Facts in 60 Minutes: The 5th Edition","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=72Lei7uug8c"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This album became the accidental home of the first hidden track, \"Her Majesty\". The track was initially intended to be removed from the final cut by Paul McCartney, but due to Abbey Road Studios' policy of leaving edited material at the end of the master tape and a subsequent oversight by the mastering engineer, it was inadvertently included after 20 seconds of silence, a surprise the band ultimately decided to keep. \"When the Beatles reviewed the master and were surprised by that song coming out of the silence, they thought it was really cool. Leave it in, they said. And so the first hidden track came to be as the result of an accident.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Hidden Tracks and Other Sundry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J_VEOIwavtg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"50th Anniversary Box Set","body":"This album was part of a series of 50th-anniversary collections by Apple Records, remastered by Giles Martin.","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"The Slate Culture Gift Guide","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Biggest Vinyl Seller of 2010s","body":"\"Abbey Road\" was the best-selling vinyl record of the 2010s, highlighting the format's unexpected resurgence. Its 